 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Interactive Slice and Point-Based Volume Rendering](4591-interactive-slice-and-point-based-volume-rendering.md)
-This method renders a 3D segmentation volume by dynamically extracting a 2D slice from the volume data, uploading it to a 2D texture, and drawing a slice plane that samples it, while overlaying segmentation labels as 3D points at voxel coordinates. It differs from standard texture slicing by decoupling the volumetric intensity visualization (the slice) from the discrete label visualization (the points), allowing for distinct rendering pipelines and per-label coloring.
+Renders a 3D segmentation volume by dynamically extracting a 2D slice, uploading it to a 2D texture, and drawing a slice plane, while overlaying segmentation labels as 3D points. It differs by decoupling volumetric intensity from discrete labels.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** Rendering time scales with the number of slices (`SLICE_COUNT`). Higher slice counts yield better quality but slower rendering [S1, unofficial]. Precomputing gradients requires more memory but can speed up rendering [S1, unofficial].
-- **Memory Complexity:** Requires memory for the volume texture, transfer function texture, and optionally a gradient texture when `PRECOMPUTE_GRADIENT` is true [S1, unofficial].
-- **Performance:** GLSL mode supports real-time lighting with up to 4 lights [S1, unofficial]. Non-GLSL mode is limited to precomputed lighting [S1, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** Real-time lighting and transfer functions in GLSL mode; flexible geometry generation.
-- **Limitations:** Requires OpenGL 3D texture or GLSL support; precomputed gradients consume additional memory and time [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Rendering time is linear with respect to the number of slices $N$ and the resolution of the slices. Higher `SLICE_COUNT` improves quality but increases rendering time [S1, unofficial][S2].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires memory for the volume texture, the generated slice geometry, and optionally a precomputed gradient texture (if `PRECOMPUTE_GRADIENT` is true) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** Supports real-time lighting with up to 4 lights. Precomputing gradients can speed up rendering at the cost of additional memory [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Real-time lighting and transfer functions; dynamic viewport alignment; flexible GLSL integration [S1, unofficial][S2].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires OpenGL 3D texture support (`Has_Texture_3D`) or GLSL support (`Has_GLSL`); non-GLSL mode lacks dynamic lighting [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** Real-time volume visualization with complex lighting and transfer functions.
+*   **When not to use:** When hardware lacks 3D texture or GLSL support; when static precomputed lighting is sufficient and dynamic lighting is not needed.
 
 ## Background
-The method is implemented in the Visualization Library (`vl::SlicedVolume`) and demonstrated in tutorials for rendering volumes with transfer functions and lighting [S1, unofficial]. It builds upon the general principles of slice geometry generation, adding specific support for dynamic lighting and transfer function evaluation via shaders or precomputation [S1, unofficial].
+Sliced Volume Rendering was introduced as part of the Visualization Library (vl) framework. It is implemented via the `vl::SlicedVolume` class, which acts as an `ActorEventCallback` to generate viewport-aligned slice geometry on the fly [S2]. The method allows for virtually any visual effect through user-specified GLSL programs, supporting per-pixel lighting and transfer functions [S2]. It is documented in the "Sliced Volume Rendering with Transfer Functions and Lighting Tutorial" [S1, unofficial].
 
